---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { site } from "../config";

const description = `How ${site.name} started: a ceiling fan, an all-night prototype, and a ball of light trying to get home.`;
---

<Base title="How it started" description={description}>
  <article class="doc">
    <p class="kicker">Origin</p>
    <h1>How it started</h1>
    <p class="note">{site.publisher}</p>

    <h2>The fan</h2>
    <p>
      After hanging out with some friends, I couldn't sleep, and I had an itch to build a game. I
      had just shipped <a href="https://wordvoyager.app/">Word Voyager</a>, but I wanted something
      more strategic and casual, in the vein of Flappy Bird and Angry Birds. Lying in bed, staring
      around the room, I looked at the fan and thought: what if I pitched a ball through that?
      Then, what if it was a giant windmill you throw a ball through?
    </p>

    <h2>The all-nighter</h2>
    <p>
      I pulled out my phone and asked ChatGPT to find a similar game. Nothing good. I checked the
      App Store. Nothing good. So I prototyped it. I stayed up all night building a game where you
      throw a ball through a rotating fan blade, then two blades, then three. I tried it. I was
      addicted already.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/prototype">Play that first prototype</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>A character</h2>
    <p>
      By morning, my ADHD hyper-fixation was in overdrive. I called a friend, showed him the
      prototype, and said: what if the ball had a character? A light form trying to pass through
      different obstacles. We spent the next couple of hours, with ChatGPT in the mix, coming up
      with a life of worlds and obstacles. After burning food I forgot on the grill, I sat down and
      started coding my way through the game.
    </p>

    <h2>Spark</h2>
    <p>
      What started as a ball pitched through some blades turned into Spark. A living ball of light,
      trapped on Earth, trying to jump through portals blocked by obstacles. It has one goal: make
      it back home to its planet, Luma.
    </p>

    <h2>Twenty worlds</h2>
    <p>
      I felt pretty proud. I worked on it for weeks. I started with 10 worlds, and after enough
      trial and error I was finishing the game too fast. So I doubled the worlds and threw in more
      obstacles: a vanishing portal, lasers in space, asteroids, and satellites you bounce off.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
